WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.350
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: My
name's Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.225
I'm a product manager
at Google, and I'm here

00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:06.880
with Maya Kaczorowski, also
a product manager at Google.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.700
And she knows everything
about security--

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.570
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I certainly
don't know everything

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:10.620
about security.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.100
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Enough that we're

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.070
going to talk about security.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:12.930
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sounds good.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.600
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Especially the
security vulnerabilities space

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.610
around Kubernetes.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.422
What interesting vulnerabilities
have you seen recently?

00:00:19.422 --> 00:00:21.630
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah, in
the last couple of months,

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.340
we've had a couple
of interesting vulns.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.950
So let's look at 2019.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.090
There was a RunC vulnerability
earlier this year,

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.510
which was, effectively,
a container escaped.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:35.070
And then more recently,
the Alpine images had a--

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.690
newer versions have a
root default password,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.488
which is kind of scary-looking.

00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:42.030
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And what was
that password, just so we know?

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:42.900
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I
think it was just null.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:43.580
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I
think it was null.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.840
I think you're exactly right.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.230
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah,
which is terrifying.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.130
And then obviously,
we've also had

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.290
some additional speculative
execution vulnerabilities

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.332
that have been announced.

00:00:50.332 --> 00:00:52.753
So lots of things affecting
the kernel and OSs

00:00:52.753 --> 00:00:54.170
and the Kubernetes
space recently.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.650
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
and what are we learning?

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.510
Are we learning
anything from this,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.180
trying to make Kubernetes
and our space better?

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.680
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I think one
thing we've really realized

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.840
is that Kubernetes is actually
really good at managing patches

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.510
and vulnerabilities, right?

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.093
Kubernetes has a
product security team

00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:08.130
that handles
Kubernetes-specific vulns.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.740
And then probably
your cloud provider

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.610
is managing a lot of
that stuff for you.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.500
On GCP, we manage
the patches for you,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.333
and you can check out
the security bulletins

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:16.700
to see how you've been patched.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.325
But we're also seeing
a lot of interest

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:20.040
from security
researchers in the space

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.080
and seeing what's
going on and can they

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.510
exploit these vulnerabilities
in live environments?

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.353
So the RunC vuln had
proofs of concept

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.770
available within
a matter of days.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.610
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.540
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And the--

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.400
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And how
quickly were patches available?

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.280
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Patches were available--

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.465
so it was embargoed, and
at announcement the patches

00:01:35.465 --> 00:01:36.090
were available.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.500
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK, good, good.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.720
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
the Alpine images,

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.500
a security researcher recently
found out that 20% of them--

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.880
20% of the top Docker Hub images
were using the null password,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.512
which is absolutely terrifying.

00:01:49.512 --> 00:01:51.690
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Wow.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.260
So that's Kubernetes itself.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.940
What about the applications
inside of Kubernetes?

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.450
How are we approaching this in
a modern containerized software

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.260
world that--

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.177
I don't know, hopefully
is a little bit better

00:02:03.177 --> 00:02:05.060
than prior generations
of software?

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:05.950
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah,
that's a good point.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.160
I think people just-- they
think of attacks on containers

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.327
as being only one category.

00:02:09.327 --> 00:02:11.910
But you can split it up between
attacks on the infrastructure,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.490
like towards the Kubernetes
API server, versus attacks

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.003
on applications
which just happen

00:02:16.003 --> 00:02:17.170
to be running in containers.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:17.630
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.420
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
so for applications

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.980
that happen to be
running in containers,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.688
we're not seeing
attacks in the wild that

00:02:21.688 --> 00:02:24.600
are significantly different
or significantly more common

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.743
than you would see in
any other environment.

00:02:26.743 --> 00:02:28.410
What's happening is
people are looking--

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.493
attackers are lazy, right?

00:02:29.493 --> 00:02:30.960
They're looking
for vulnerabilities

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.628
that have been
unpatched for years

00:02:32.628 --> 00:02:34.170
and seeing if they
can exploit those,

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.750
and they don't seem to
care whether or not it's

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.683
a containerized application.

00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:40.350
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Would
it be fair to say

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.130
that modern CI/CD and the
way we use that in Kubernetes

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:49.242
should be helping our ability
to patch safely and reliably?

00:02:49.242 --> 00:02:50.700
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
It should, right.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.770
If you think about
a container, you're

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.312
not patching a live container.

00:02:54.312 --> 00:02:56.520
You're actually patching a
container that sits in a--

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.980
an image that sits in a
registry and then rebuilding

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.180
and redeploying that container.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.390
So patching becomes
as easy for you

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.233
to implement as your typical--

00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:06.400
your normal rollout process.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.880
So you can actually patch
something with the same canary

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.890
testing, deployment,
all those tools

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.550
that you would use anyway and
make it much safer than you

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.490
would otherwise.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.668
What that should mean
in theory, although not

00:03:16.668 --> 00:03:18.960
always in practice, is that
you should be able to patch

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.090
more often and patch more
easily than you would've

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.360
been able to do before.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.920
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And do you
think we're seeing that yet?

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.295
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
I think we are.

00:03:25.295 --> 00:03:27.480
I hear of some customers
who are tearing down

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.140
their entire environment and
redeploying it every x hours, x

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.462
days, for sure.

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:34.170
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right,
GKE and security,

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.128
what are some of the
things we're doing to push

00:03:36.128 --> 00:03:37.315
the envelope in that space?

00:03:37.315 --> 00:03:39.690
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure, so at
our recent Next conference,

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.712
Google Cloud announced
a couple of things.

00:03:41.712 --> 00:03:43.170
We have binary
authorization, which

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.958
is our way of enforcing
that containers

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.500
meet certain
requirements before they

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.120
get deployed to production.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.578
CGR vulnerability
scanning, this is

00:03:49.578 --> 00:03:51.078
related to what we
were just talking

00:03:51.078 --> 00:03:53.036
about around patching
things before they end up

00:03:53.036 --> 00:03:55.370
in production so you actually
can apply certain patches.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.150
It lets you scan images
in your repository

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.500
for known vulnerabilities.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.560
And we just
announced GKE Sandbox

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.070
in beta, which is using Google's
gVisor technology to help you

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.690
isolate untrusted code
in your environment

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.420
in multi-tenant environments.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.100
And of course, we're
working on more

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.140
but I don't have anything
to share right away.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:13.740
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All right.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:14.940
Maya, thank you very much.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.440
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Thank you so much.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

